    <innernamespace refid="namespaceapi_1_1v2_1_1Types">api::v2::Types</innernamespace>
    <briefdescription>
<para>Login, credential and identity data types used by the gatekeeper subsystem. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>This header defines two small plain-data structs that act as &quot;containers&quot; passed between the login, session and token helpers. <ref refid="structCredentials" kindref="compound">Credentials</ref> holds the raw username/password typed by a client. <ref refid="structClientLoginData" kindref="compound">ClientLoginData</ref> is a larger bag of optional fields that grows as a login or authentication request moves through the system: some fields come from the request, some from the database, and some are produced when a token is created. Nothing here does any work; it just gives the different helpers a shared vocabulary for the data they exchange.</para>
<para>WHY THIS FILE MATTERS IN THE SERVER: This file belongs to the gatekeeper (the security/identity part of the server). <ref refid="controller_8hpp_1a3100d7dd64f64ce2844ac60863afbaa1" kindref="member">GateKeeper</ref> reads the request body into <ref refid="structCredentials" kindref="compound">Credentials</ref>, then creates a <ref refid="structClientLoginData" kindref="compound">ClientLoginData</ref> and passes it to <ref refid="sessionmanager_8cpp_1aa7df7e3b388a985439e1eb11eba04a0f" kindref="member">SessionManager</ref> (session database rows) and <ref refid="tokenmanager_8cpp_1a29cda383d518dafdbd3cf3438adea93f" kindref="member">TokenManager</ref> (JWT creation/validation). Almost every security function takes a <ref refid="structClientLoginData" kindref="compound">ClientLoginData</ref> by reference, so without these types the helpers would need long, duplicated parameter lists and could not share state. Fields are optional because they are filled in at different stages - a field that is <computeroutput>nullopt</computeroutput> simply means &quot;not known yet&quot; at this stage of the pipeline.</para>
<para>CONCEPTS TO LEARN (for beginners):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>struct: <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/classes">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/classes</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>std::optional (&quot;a value that may be missing&quot;): <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/optional">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/optional</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>std::chrono (time durations/time points): <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/chrono">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/chrono</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>JWT (a signed token carrying identity claims): <ulink url="https://jwt.io/introduction">https://jwt.io/introduction</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>std::uint64_t (a fixed 64-bit whole number): <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/types/integer">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/types/integer</ulink></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="author"><para>Amr Osman <ulink url="mailto:dr3mro@gmail.com">dr3mro@gmail.com</ulink> </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="date"><para>2026-11-16 </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
    </detaileddescription>
